#
# ================ ROW 1 (rev59 Major, Critic M-1): mstatus.MIE must not mask an NMI expectation ============
# rtl/ibex_controller.sv:498-499 is
#   assign handle_irq = ~debug_mode_q & ~debug_single_step_i & ~nmi_mode_q &
#                       (irq_nm | (irq_pending_i & irq_enabled)) & ...
# with irq_enabled = csr_mstatus_mie_i | (priv_mode_i == PRIV_LVL_U) at :490. irq_nm is ORed OUTSIDE
# irq_enabled, so an NMI is takeable in M mode with MIE clear. Landing 52 applied the MIE term to the whole
# expectation queue, so in every regular handler, from reset until a program enables interrupts, and under the
# with_nmi storm mix, a withheld NMI's bound could not expire at all.
# THE FIX: the mask is decided per expectation, and the MIE term is qualified on !expects[i].nmi.
# The sibling end-of-run arm at :279 needed no change: its condition already reads
# expects[i].nmi || last_st.mstatus[MIE] || last_st.prv != M, so it has always exempted an NMI
# expectation from the enable, and the in-run mask now agrees with it instead of contradicting it.
#
# RED A. Fixture: gen_ut_irq_nmi_long (committed) on the reset-reads program, which never writes mstatus, so
# MIE is clear for the whole run and the pre-fix mask is active on every record. The test raises the NMI at
# retired record 200 and holds it UNTIL_TAKEN. Mutation MUT-NMIWITHHOLD: gen_dut_top.sv's .irq_nm_i tied to
# 1'b0, so the pin reaches the checker and never the core - a genuine never-taken NMI.
#   $ bash <root>/dv/auto_dv/tb/gen_tb_local.sh run <root>/dv/auto_dv/out_<b> <name> \
#       dv.auto_dv.gen_tb.gen_tests.gen_ut_irq_nmi_long \
#       +gen_mem_image=<scratch>/irq1b/rrprog/prog.vmem +gen_mem_image_crc32=69379b6e +gen_mem_image_words=24 \
#       +gen_boot_addr=80000000 +gen_tohost_addr=800000c0 +gen_fetch_en_at_reset=0
#     (image md5 847593fd93e4da76b1dc1008dc3e73dd; the crc32 plusarg is the value the l47 log records for this
#      image, not a recomputation - a naive little-endian crc32 of the vmem words gives d0ee4d11 and fatals)
#     verdict from <out>/<name>/verdict.txt, never from an exit code.
#
#   build                          nmi_entry   real UVM_ERROR   verdict reason
#   PRE  + MUT-NMIWITHHOLD              0            0          cocotb_summary at stdout.log:54
#   POST + MUT-NMIWITHHOLD              1            1          uvm_error at sim.log:33
#   POST + MUT + gen_chk_nmi_entry=0    0            0          cocotb_summary at stdout.log:54
#   PRE  clean (ablation)               0            2          uvm_error at sim.log:35
#   POST clean (ablation)               0            2          uvm_error at sim.log:35
#
# THE PRE-FIX MUTANT RUN CARRIES ZERO UVM_ERRORS OF ANY KIND. A never-taken NMI produced no report at all; the
# only thing that failed was the test's own timeout, "GEN_UT_IRQ_NMI_LONG: the NMI was not taken within 4000
# cycles". After the fix the same run fails on the checker instead, which is the whole point of having one.
# The fired line, verbatim:
#   [nmi_entry] lines 40000 (enable bits 00000000) raised at cycle 1361 (order 200) not taken within 17
#   records (now order 218, takeable records 18, mie 00000000 mstatus 00000080)
# Three readings of it. "enable bits 00000000" is correct and not a defect: the NMI has no mie bit, and the
# helper maps lines 0..17 only. "mstatus 00000080" is MPIE alone, MIE CLEAR, which is the state the Major says
# must still be judged. And this is the SAME cycle and the SAME two order numbers the Critic quotes for the
# pre-landing-52 checker on this fixture, so the fix restores exactly the behaviour landing 52 removed rather
# than inventing a new one.
# CARRIED FORWARD, the DV Lead's second acceptance point in full: the NMI path is takeable with MIE clear,
# so it is not folded into the enable mask here AND it must not be folded into the regime-independent
# end-of-test expectation I take after this landing. That expectation reconciles every raised enabled line
# against the entries at end of test; the NMI's enable is not mstatus.MIE and it has no mie bit at all, so
# its reconciliation term is nmi_mode and debug mode only. Written here because the constraint is easy to
# lose between two landings.
# THE TWO CLEAN CONTROLS ARE NOT ERROR-FREE AND I AM NOT CALLING THEM CLEAN. Both carry two [double_fault]
# errors, because the reset-reads program installs no NMI handler and the NMI vector at 0x8000007c lies below
# this linker script's PROG origin 0x80000080. The test ITSELF passes in both (GEN_UT_IRQ_NMI_LONG_PASS,
# "retired 400 mismatches 0"), so the NMI is taken on a clean build; the statistic the control carries is
# nmi_entry 0, and the double faults are a property of the fixture, present identically before and after.
#
# ================ ROW 2 (rev59 Medium, Critic M-2): the bound accrues, it does not restart ==================
# Landing 52's comment said the bound "accrues only over records in which the interrupt could be taken" and
# the code set order_at = st.order on every masked record, which DISCARDS the records already spent. Under
# that shape the bound fires only on 18 or more CONSECUTIVE unmasked records, so a regime whose masked records
# recur more often than the bound cannot be judged at all whatever the DUT does. That is the vacuity the DV
# Lead ruled on the storm case in gen_fu_l52_vacuity_corrigendum.log, and it is the same defect seen from the
# other side.
# THE FIX: expect_t carries an unmasked count. It increments once per record in which THAT expectation could
# have been taken, it is reset where the entry restart already resets the baseline (:112), and it is what the
# bound compares. order_at now only carries the order of the last restart, for the message.
#
# THE CASE FOR ACCRUAL RATHER THAN REWORDING THE COMMENT TO SAY THE BOUND RESTARTS, which is the alternative
# rev59 offered. The property worth having is that an enabled raised line is taken within N records in which it
# could have been taken. Accrual states exactly that. A restart states instead: within N records since the last
# record in which it could NOT be taken, which is not a bound at all when masked records are periodic: every
# handler entry clears MIE, so any
# interrupt-carrying regime resets the clock forever. The DV Lead's acceptance point is the same case from the
# plan side: cp_mie_global_edge's set_pending bin asserts that when software sets MIE with a line pending the
# entry follows before the next retirement, so an interrupt withheld across a software MIE toggle is
# plan-asserted behaviour, and only the accrued form can judge it. I chose accrual and the plan needs no
# sentence excusing the checker.
#
# RED B. Fixture: gen_ut_lockstep (committed) on a scratch program that alternates mstatus.MIE in six-record
# windows - six takeable records, then six masked, six times over, thirty-six takeable records against a bound
# of seventeen with no takeable run ever longer than six. Source
# <scratch>/l54/gen_irq_mie_toggle.S md5 25a722a9fc12da2c5dbca660a1e8ec09; image built by
#   $ python3 dv/auto_dv/stim/gen_program.py --seed 1 --out <scratch>/l54/tprog \
#       --directed <scratch>/l54/gen_irq_mie_toggle.S --gcc-opts=-Idv/auto_dv/tests/gen_programs
#   OK ... entry=0x80000080 words=36 crc32=0x3b4f1c71   (image md5 3bb833ad1d27ecaa51f8dd9cf65244af)
# Mutation MUT-IRQWITHHOLDALL: gen_dut_top.sv's .irq_software_i, .irq_timer_i, .irq_external_i and .irq_fast_i
# all tied low, so whichever line the storm raises reaches the checker and never the core.
#   $ bash <root>/dv/auto_dv/tb/gen_tb_local.sh run <root>/dv/auto_dv/out_<b> <name> \
#       dv.auto_dv.gen_tb.gen_tests.gen_ut_lockstep \
#       +gen_mem_image=<scratch>/l54/tprog/prog.vmem +gen_mem_image_crc32=3b4f1c71 +gen_mem_image_words=36 \
#       +gen_boot_addr=80000000 +gen_tohost_addr=800000f0 +gen_fetch_en_at_reset=0 +gen_ut_boot_retire=80 \
#       +gen_knob_irq_regime=storm +gen_knob_irq_hold=until_taken
#
#   build                          irq_entry   real UVM_ERROR   composition
#   PRE  + MUT-IRQWITHHOLDALL           0           408         408 [irq_pending]
#   POST + MUT-IRQWITHHOLDALL           1           409         408 [irq_pending] + 1 [irq_entry]
#   POST + MUT + gen_chk_irq_entry=0    0           408         408 [irq_pending]
#   PRE  clean (ablation)               0             1         1 [double_fault]
#   POST clean (ablation)               0             1         1 [double_fault]
#
# The accounting is exact: 408 to 409 to 408, so the named rule is precisely the difference and nothing else
# moved. The 408 [irq_pending] are the mutation's own signature and are present in both mutant builds - the
# pins are raised, the model's pending says yes and the DUT's irq_pending_o says no. They are collateral of
# tying the inputs low, not a finding.
# The fired line, verbatim, and it contains the demonstration:
#   [irq_entry] lines 00100 (enable bits 00200000) raised at cycle 181 (order 40) not taken within 17 records
#   (now order 82, takeable records 18, mie 7fff0888 mstatus 00000088)
# Order 40 to order 82 is FORTY-TWO records elapsed, of which EIGHTEEN were takeable. The pre-fix code needed
# eighteen consecutive and the longest run here is six, which is why the same mutant produces nothing on the
# PRE build. The two semantics are separated numerically by the message itself. (lines 00100 is line 8, fast[5],
# whose enable is bit 21: 0x00200000. mie reads 7fff0888 because the program writes -1 and the WARL mask keeps
# only the implemented lines.)
#
# ================ ROW 3 (Critic L-2): two RTL withholding terms, named exclusions with an observable ========
# :498 has two further terms the mask does not carry. Single step, ~debug_single_step_i, driven from
# dcsr_q.step at rtl/ibex_cs_registers.sv:1038, bit 2 of the packed dcsr_t at :217-233. And the Zcmp expansion
# commit phase, which needs micro-op records gen_model_state does not carry.
# I did NOT mask on single step, and the reason is not convenience: st.dcsr is the ISA model's copy, read with
# gen_isa_read_csr, and it is not the DUT's debug_single_step_i. Masking a checker on a mirror I have not
# proved tracks the pin would be exactly the shape this landing exists to remove - a mask that can silence the
# check without anything reporting it. Instead the checker COUNTS the records in which the model's step bit is
# set and prints the count in its summary, so the exclusion is measured rather than assumed and the first
# fixture that drives single step will show a nonzero count instead of quietly losing coverage.
# MEASURED: 0 in all six regression smokes below, including the debug-request storm. Stated precisely, that is
# a statement about the model's dcsr step bit in these fixtures, not a statement that the DUT never single-stepped.
# Zcmp stays a named exclusion with nothing to count, because the state is not in the record. No committed
# fixture drives interrupts across a Zcmp expansion today (gen_zcmp_irq_directed.S has no testlist entry).
#
# ================ ROW 4 (rev59 Low, Critic L-3): the mret invariant, and what it actually guards ============
# gen_fcov_pkg.sv:505 recorded prev_trapped = st.is_trap. The Critic found the false-positive path: a trap
# taken INSIDE debug mode leaves MIE untouched, because the clear at rtl/ibex_cs_registers.sv:924 sits inside
# "else if (!debug_mode_i)" at :918, so such a record publishes MIE set, and an mret after it with a line
# pending would raise GEN_FCOV_REF on a legitimate booking. Recorded now as st.is_trap && !st.debug_mode.
# THE DV LEAD'S T6 CORRECTION, and it is only true once that change is in. Both arms of the invariant are
# structurally zero against the current classifier: the is_intr arm because :449 forces was to 0 on a non-debug
# entry, and the trapped-predecessor arm because the published state is post-step, so a non-debug trap's own
# record already carries the MIE clear. The debug-mode trap was the single residue where the second arm could
# fire, and excluding it closes the gap the DV Lead's sentence assumes.
# SAID PLAINLY, BECAUSE IT IS THE THIRD CHECK OF THIS SHAPE THIS WEEK: after this change the referee CANNOT
# fire on any DUT behaviour. It guards the :449 classifier and the post-step publication assumption, and a
# nonzero count is a regression in one of those two, never a design bug. It is not a measurement of the model
# and this log does not let it be read as one. The comment at :470-478 now says so in the source.
#
